ite, eventually two different keys will inevitably map to the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere are the primary ways hash maps handle these collisions and how they impact performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Separate Chaining (Open Addressing)\nThis is the most common strategy. Each \"slot\" in the array does not store the data directly; instead, it points to a secondary data structure (usually a **Linked List** or a balanced search tree).\n\n*   **How it works:** When a collision occurs at index 5, the hash map simply appends the new key-value pair to the list at index 5.\n*   **Lookup process:** The map hashes the key, goes to the index, and then traverses the list linearly until it finds the matching key.\n\n### 2. Open Addressing (Probing)\nIn this method, all elements stay within the main array itself. If a collision occurs, the map looks for the next available empty slot.\n\n*   **Linear Probing:** It checks the very next slot ($index+1, index+2$, etc.) until it finds a hole.\n*   **Quadratic Probing:** It uses a mathematical formula to find the next slot ($index+1^2, index+2^2$, etc.) to avoid \"clumping\" data together.\n*   **Double Hashing:** It uses a second hash function to determine the"}, {"num_denoising_steps": 26, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 26, "decoder_forward_passes": [26], "tokens_per_forward": [19.69230842590332], "prefill_seconds": 0.2552435829998103, "canvas_seconds": [0.30685912199987797, 0.28585161199998765], "total_seconds": 0.8509506069999588, "generated_tokens": 512, "canvas_tokens": 512, "tokens_per_second": 859.4828990968425, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 859.4828990968425, "seconds_per_step": 0.022911808615390328, "token_checksum": 25288894, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, you first have to understand why they happen.\n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string) into an integer index in an array. Because the number of possible keys is effectively infinite but the size of the array is finite, two different keys will eventually map to the same index. This event is a **collision**.\n\nHere is an explanation of how maps resolve these collisions and how it impacts performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Separate Chaining (Open Addressing)\nThis is the most common method. Instead of storing a single value at each array index, each index (often called a \"bucket\") points to a secondary data structure, usually a **Linked List**.\n\n*   **How it works:** When a collision occurs at index 5, the new key-value pair is simply appended to the list at index 5.\n*   **Lookup process:** The map hashes the key to find index 5, then it traverses the linked list linearly until it finds the matching key.\n*   **Pros:** Very easy to implement; the map never \"fills up\" (it just gets slower).\n*   **Cons:** If many keys hash to the same index, the list becomes long, degrading performance.\n\n### 2. Open Addressing (Probing)\nIn this method, all elements are stored directly in the array. If a collision occurs, the map looks for the next available \"empty\" slot.\n\n*   **Linear Probing:** If index $i$ is full, check $i+1$, then $i+2$, and so on.\n*   **Quadratic Probing:** If index $i$ is full, check $i+1^2$, then $i+2^2$"}, {"num_denoising_steps": 32, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 32, "decoder_forward_passes": [32], "tokens_per_forward": [16.0], "prefill_seconds": 0.04951112899971122, "canvas_seconds": [0.32925835099968026, 0.3942301289998795], "total_seconds": 0.7758518190003088, "generated_tokens": 512, "canvas_tokens": 512, "tokens_per_second": 704.9033697941097, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 704.9033697941097, "seconds_per_step": 0.022698146562518673, "token_checksum": 24494930, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, we first have to understand why they happen.\n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string) into an index (an integer). Because the number of possible keys is often infinite but the size of the internal array is finite, two different keys will eventually map to the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere is how hash maps resolve these conflicts and the resulting impact on performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Collision Resolution Strategies\n\n#### A. Chaining (Separate Addressing)\nThis is the most common method. Instead of storing a single value directly at each array index, each slot in the array points to another data structure (usually a **Linked List** or a **Balanced Tree**).\n\n*   **How it works:** When two keys collide at index 5, the second key-value pair is simply appended to the list located at index 5.\n*   **Lookup process:** To find a value, you hash the key to find the index, then traverse the linked list at that index until you find the matching key.\n\n#### B. Open Addressing\nIn this method, all elements are stored directly within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map looks for the next \"empty\" slot based on a specific strategy:\n\n*   **Linear Probing:** If index $i$ is full, check $i+1$, then $i+2$, and so on.\n*   **Quadratic Probing:** If index $i$ is full, check $i+1^2$, then $i+2^2$, etc., to avoid \"clustering.\"\n*   **Double Hashing:** Use a second hash function to determ"}]}
{"benchmark": "diffusion", "args": {"family": "diffusion", "model": "google/diffusiongemma-26B-A4B-it", "device": "auto", "dtype": "bfloat16", "compile": "auto", "expert_parallel": true, "experts_implementation": "by_token_count", "batch_sizes": [32], "variant_batch_size": 1, "variants": ["check1", "check0"], "prompts": "chat", "prompt_length": 64, "prompt_bucket": 64, "max_new_tokens": 512, "stop_check_interval": 1, "warmup_runs": 2, "timed_runs": 3, "gsm8k_examples": 0, "gsm8k_batch_size": 10, "seed": 0, "label": "diffusion_b1_check1", "output": "/opt/dgemma/repo/results/session2/results.jsonl", "batch_size": 1, "sampler": "static", "cache": "static", "max_denoising_steps": null, "attn_implementation": null, "do_sample": false}, "environment": {"torch": "2.15.0+cpu", "transformers": "5.22.2", "python": "3.12.16", "device": "tpu", "timestamp": "2026-11-10T12:45:42Z", "torch_tpu": "unknown", "device_count": 1}, "load_seconds": 18.124492779999855, "warmup_seconds": [114.53753794899967, 0.5612459550002313], "median_tokens_per_second": 802.6884771257653, "median_canvas_tokens_per_second": 802.6884771257653, "median_total_seconds": 0.6567489320000277, "peak_memory_bytes": 27419771392, "runs": [{"num_denoising_steps": 28, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 28, "decoder_forward_passes": [28], "tokens_per_forward": [18.285715103149414], "prefill_seconds": 0.01889251000011427, "canvas_seconds": [0.371470828999918, 0.26406745299982504], "total_seconds": 0.6567489320000277, "generated_tokens": 512, "canvas_tokens": 512, "tokens_per_second": 802.6884771257653, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 802.6884771257653, "seconds_per_step": 0.022780586499996907, "token_checksum": 25138763, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, you first have to understand why they happen.\n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string) into an integer index in an array. Because the number of possible keys is infinitely large but the array size is finite, eventually two different keys will inevitably map to the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere are the primary ways hash maps handle these collisions and how they impact performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Separate Chaining (Open Addressing)\nThis is the most common strategy. Each \"slot\" in the array does not store the data directly; instead, it points to a secondary data structure (usually a **Linked List** or a balanced search tree).\n\n*   **How it works:** When a collision occurs at index 5, the hash map simply appends the new key-value pair to the list at index 5.\n*   **Lookup process:** The map hashes the key, goes to the index, and then traverses the list linearly until it finds the matching key.\n\n### 2. Open Addressing (Probing)\nIn this method, all elements stay within the main array itself. If a collision occurs, the map looks for the next available empty slot.\n\n*   **Linear Probing:** It checks the very next slot ($index+1, index+2$, etc.) until it finds a hole.\n*   **Quadratic Probing:** It uses a mathematical formula to find the next slot ($index+1^2, index+2^2$, etc.) to avoid \"clumping\" data together.\n*   **Double Hashing:** It uses a second hash function to determine the"}, {"num_denoising_steps": 26, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 26, "decoder_forward_passes": [26], "tokens_per_forward": [19.69230842590332], "prefill_seconds": 0.01956134900001416, "canvas_seconds": [0.30707243200004086, 0.28622453199977826], "total_seconds": 0.6157828629998221, "generated_tokens": 512, "canvas_tokens": 512, "tokens_per_second": 858.7412362314805, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 858.7412362314805, "seconds_per_step": 0.022931596692300306, "token_checksum": 25288894, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, you first have to understand why they happen.\n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string) into an integer index in an array. Because the number of possible keys is effectively infinite but the size of the array is finite, two different keys will eventually map to the same index. This event is a **collision**.\n\nHere is an explanation of how maps resolve these collisions and how it impacts performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Separate Chaining (Open Addressing)\nThis is the most common method. Instead of storing a single value at each array index, each index (often called a \"bucket\") points to a secondary data structure, usually a **Linked List**.\n\n*   **How it works:** When a collision occurs at index 5, the new key-value pair is simply appended to the list at index 5.\n*   **Lookup process:** The map hashes the key to find index 5, then it traverses the linked list linearly until it finds the matching key.\n*   **Pros:** Very easy to implement; the map never \"fills up\" (it just gets slower).\n*   **Cons:** If many keys hash to the same index, the list becomes long, degrading performance.\n\n### 2. Open Addressing (Probing)\nIn this method, all elements are stored directly in the array. If a collision occurs, the map looks for the next available \"empty\" slot.\n\n*   **Linear Probing:** If index $i$ is full, check $i+1$, then $i+2$, and so on.\n*   **Quadratic Probing:** If index $i$ is full, check $i+1^2$, then $i+2^2$"}, {"num_denoising_steps": 32, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 32, "decoder_forward_passes": [32], "tokens_per_forward": [16.0], "prefill_seconds": 0.04481387900023037, "canvas_seconds": [0.32919452100031776, 0.3946439289998125], "total_seconds": 0.7714877290000004, "generated_tokens": 512, "canvas_tokens": 512, "tokens_per_second": 704.5801909620967, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 704.5801909620967, "seconds_per_step": 0.022708557812492813, "token_checksum": 24494930, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, we first have to understand why they happen.\n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string) into an index (an integer). Because the number of possible keys is often infinite but the size of the internal array is finite, two different keys will eventually map to the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere is how hash maps resolve these conflicts and the resulting impact on performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Collision Resolution Strategies\n\n#### A. Chaining (Separate Addressing)\nThis is the most common method. Instead of storing a single value directly at each array index, each slot in the array points to another data structure (usually a **Linked List** or a **Balanced Tree**).\n\n*   **How it works:** When two keys collide at index 5, the second key-value pair is simply appended to the list located at index 5.\n*   **Lookup process:** To find a value, you hash the key to find the index, then traverse the linked list at that index until you find the matching key.\n\n#### B. Open Addressing\nIn this method, all elements are stored directly within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map looks for the next \"empty\" slot based on a specific strategy:\n\n*   **Linear Probing:** If index $i$ is full, check $i+1$, then $i+2$, and so on.\n*   **Quadratic Probing:** If index $i$ is full, check $i+1^2$, then $i+2^2$, etc., to avoid \"clustering.\"\n*   **Double Hashing:** Use a second hash function to determ"}]}
