{"benchmark": "diffusion", "args": {"family": "diffusion", "model": "google/diffusiongemma-26B-A4B-it", "device": "auto", "dtype": "bfloat16", "compile": "auto", "expert_parallel": true, "experts_implementation": "by_token_count", "batch_sizes": [32], "variant_batch_size": 1, "variants": ["check1", "check0"], "prompts": "chat", "prompt_length": 64, "prompt_bucket": 64, "max_new_tokens": 512, "stop_check_interval": 0, "warmup_runs": 2, "timed_runs": 3, "gsm8k_examples": 0, "gsm8k_batch_size": 10, "seed": 0, "label": "diffusion_b1_check0", "output": "/opt/dgemma/repo/results/session2/results.jsonl", "batch_size": 1, "sampler": "static", "cache": "static", "max_denoising_steps": null, "attn_implementation": null, "do_sample": false}, "environment": {"torch": "2.15.0+cpu", "transformers": "5.22.2", "python": "3.12.16", "device": "tpu", "timestamp": "2026-11-10T12:46:31Z", "torch_tpu": "unknown", "device_count": 1}, "load_seconds": 17.12809306500003, "warmup_seconds": [28.78800593799997, 1.8867056499998398], "median_tokens_per_second": 273.9463106077479, "median_canvas_tokens_per_second": 273.9463106077479, "median_total_seconds": 2.1878563309996935, "peak_memory_bytes": 27419771392, "runs": [{"num_denoising_steps": 96, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 0, "decoder_forward_passes": [24], "tokens_per_forward": [21.33333396911621], "prefill_seconds": 0.020156488999873545, "canvas_seconds": [0.9431446150001648, 0.9220610749998741], "total_seconds": 1.8885747889999038, "generated_tokens": 512, "canvas_tokens": 512, "tokens_per_second": 274.02857272378014, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 274.02857272378014, "seconds_per_step": 0.019462690625000317, "token_checksum": 26648843, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, you first have to understand why they happen. A hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string) into an integer index in an array. \n\nBecause the number of possible keys is effectively infinite but the size of the array is finite, two different keys will eventually produce the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere are the two primary ways hash maps handle these collisions, and how they impact performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Separate Chaining (Open Hashing)\nThis is the most common resolution strategy. Instead of each slot in the array holding a single value, each slot holds a pointer to a **linked list** (or sometimes a balanced binary search tree).\n\n*   **How it works:** When a collision occurs at Index 5, the new entry is simply appended to the list located at Index 5.\n*   **Lookup:** To find a value, the map hashes the key to find Index 5, then traverses the linked list linearly to find the matching key.\n*   **Worst-Case Scenario:** If the hash function is poor, every single key could hash to the same index. The hash map effectively turns into one long linked list.\n*   **Worst-Case Lookup Time:** $O(n)$, where $n$ is the number of elements.\n\n### 2. Open Addressing (Closed Hashing)\nIn this strategy, all elements are stored within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map \"probes\" for the next empty slot based on a specific strategy.\n\n*   **Strategies:**\n    *   **Linear Probing:** If I"}, {"num_denoising_steps": 96, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 0, "decoder_forward_passes": [34], "tokens_per_forward": [15.05882453918457], "prefill_seconds": 1.0336240030001136, "canvas_seconds": [0.9426175739999962, 0.9223704850001013], "total_seconds": 2.902603361999809, "generated_tokens": 512, "canvas_tokens": 512, "tokens_per_second": 273.9463106077479, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 273.9463106077479, "seconds_per_step": 0.01946853498958016, "token_checksum": 24482583, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, you first have to understand why they happen. A hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key into an index in an internal array. Because the number of possible keys is often infinite but the size of the array is finite, two different keys will eventually map to the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere is how hash maps resolve these conflicts and how it impacts performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Collision Resolution Strategies\n\n#### A. Separate Chaining (Most Common)\nIn this strategy, each slot in the array does not store a single value. Instead, it stores a pointer to a secondary data structure (usually a **Linked List** or a balanced tree).\n*   **How it works:** When a collision occurs, the new key-value pair is simply appended to the list at that index.\n*   **Lookup process:** To find a value, the map hashes the key, goes to the index, and then traverses the list linearly to find the matching key.\n*   **Modern Optimization:** In implementations (like Java 8+), if a list at a specific index grows too large (e.g., more than 8 elements), it is converted from a Linked List into a **Red-Black Tree** to keep searches fast.\n\n#### B. Open Addressing\nIn this strategy, all elements are stored directly within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map \"probes\" for the next empty slot.\n*   **Linear Probing:** It checks the very next slot ($i+1$, $i+2$, etc.) until it finds an empty space.\n*   **Quadratic Probin"}, {"num_denoising_steps": 96, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 0, "decoder_forward_passes": [27], "tokens_per_forward": [18.962963104248047], "prefill_seconds": 0.31695049099971584, "canvas_seconds": [0.9442798050004058, 0.9226407750002181], "total_seconds": 2.1878563309996935, "generated_tokens": 512, "canvas_tokens": 512, "tokens_per_second": 273.6642267362884, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 273.6642267362884, "seconds_per_step": 0.019488602499999768, "token_checksum": 26727475, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, you first have to understand why they happen.\n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string) into an index (an integer position). Because the number of possible keys is effectively infinite but the size of the array is finite, two different keys will eventually result in the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere is an explanation of how hash maps resolve collisions and how it impacts performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Separate Chaining (Open Addressing)\nThis is the most common resolution strategy. Instead of each slot in the array holding a single value, each slot holds a **pointer to a data structure** (usually a Linked List).\n\n*   **How it works:** When a collision occurs at index 5, the new key-value pair is simply appended to the list at that index.\n*   **Lookup process:** To find a value, the map hashes the key to find the index, then traverses the linked list linearly until it finds the matching key.\n*   **Optimization:** Many modern implementations (like Java 8+) convert the linked list into a **Balanced Binary Search Tree** if the list grows too large (e.g., more than 8 elements), which improves search time from linear to logarithmic.\n\n### 2. Open Addressing (Probing)\nIn this strategy, all elements are stored directly in the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map looks for the next \"empty\" slot.\n\n*   **Linear Probing:** If index 5 is taken, it checks index 6, then 7, then 8, un"}]}
